ueblo CO","description":"...The Flood Watch continues for the following rivers in Colorado...\n\nRio Grande At Alamosa affecting Alamosa Vicinity/Central San Luis\nValley.\n\nFor the Rio Grande...including Alamosa...flooding is forecasted to\nbegin by mid week.\n\n* WHAT...Flooding is possible.\n\n* WHERE...Rio Grande at Alamosa.\n\n* WHEN...From Wednesday afternoon to Thursday evening.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 6:30 AM MST Monday the stage was 3.0 feet.\n- Forecast...Flood stage may be reached early Wednesday afternoon.\n- Flood stage is 9.0 feet.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-05T23:45:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Alamosa  Vicinity/Central San Luis Valley Below 8500 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.39e48bfa9f531d07f4b0ba1a7d1f2b72b6af2f13.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.39e48bfa9f531d07f4b0ba1a7d1f2b72b6af2f13.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued November 2 at 7:19AM MST until November 3 at 7:34PM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"...The Flood Warning is extended for the following rivers in\nColorado...\n\nRio Grande Near Del Norte affecting Rio Grande County.\n\nFor the Upper Rio Grande River...including Del Norte...Moderate\nflooding is forecast.\n\n* WHAT...Moderate flooding is occurring and moderate flooding is\nforecast.\n\n* WHERE...Rio Grande near Del Norte.\n\n* WHEN...Until Tuesday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...At 7.0 feet, Moderate Flood. Water flows into farm\nproperties and homes west of Del Norte. Serious lowland and meadow\nflooding occurs.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 7:01 AM MST Monday the stage was 6.6 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 5.0 feet.\n- Recent Activity...The maximum river stage in the 24 hours ending\nat 7:01 AM MST Monday was 6.6 feet.\n- Forecast...The river is expected to rise to a crest of 7.0 feet\nlate this afternoon. It will then fall below flood stage tomorrow\nmorning.\n- Flood stage is 5.5 feet.\n- Flood History...This crest compares to a previous crest of 6.8\nfeet on 10/05/1911.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-03T19:34:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Rio Grande, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.e9053015039ef7b205c67b91b64c8c59ba59a9c9.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.e9053015039ef7b205c67b91b64c8c59ba59a9c9.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued November 1 at 4:37PM MST until November 2 at 4:30PM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding along the South Fork Rio Grande and mainstem Rio\nGrande through South Fork caused by rain and snowmelt is expected.\n\n* WHERE...Southeastern Mineral County in south central Colorado...\nWest Central Rio Grande County in south central Colorado...\n\n* WHEN...Until 430 PM MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding along the South Fork Rio Grande and mainstem Rio\nGrande through South Fork is expected. Additional flooding of\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations is\nimminent or occurring. Numerous roads remain closed due to\nflooding. Streams continue to rise due to excess runoff from\nrainfall and upstream snowmelt.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 433 PM MST, Rain and snowmelt is occurring, and will cause\nflooding. Between 1 and 2 inches of rain have fallen on top of\nmelting snow. Stream gauge data indicates that the South Fork Rio\nGrande at South Fork will be reaching flood stage soon.\n- Additional rainfall amounts of 1 to 3 inches are possible in the\nwarned area.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nSouth Fork and areas along Highway 160 up to Wolf Creek Pass.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Mineral, CO; Rio Grande, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a1fe13ce206a35627c94a6be4c4adad3c2e93d4f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.a1fe13ce206a35627c94a6be4c4adad3c2e93d4f.001.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"PUB","issued":"2026-11-02T18:14:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KPUB 021814 AFDPUB Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Pueblo CO 1114 AM MST Mon Nov 2 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Flooding continues across rivers along the eastern San Juans and adjacent San Luis Valley. - Gusty winds and showers expected today, especially across the plains, with gusts of 30-50mph expected. - Unsettled pattern continues through the week and into the weekend.","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=PUB&issuedby=PUB&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":{"office":"PUB","issued":"2026-11-02T08:09:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FLUS45 KPUB 020809 HWOPUB Hazardous Weather Outlook National Weather Service Pueblo CO 109 AM MST 